    "cond": {
      "id": "dupuytren",
      "name": "Dupuytren's disease (Dupuytren's contracture)",
      "clin": "Dupuytren's disease / contracture (palmar fibromatosis); post-fasciectomy rehabilitation",
      "blurb": "Dupuytren's is a thickening of the tough layer of tissue just under the skin of the palm. It can form a firm lump, then a rope-like cord, and over months or years the cord may gently pull one or more fingers, most often the ring or little finger, toward the palm so they no longer straighten fully. It is not cancer, it is usually not painful, and many people live with a small lump for years without needing any treatment. When a finger does start to bend, there are established procedures that can release it, and hand therapy helps you get the most out of your hand afterwards. It can come back over time, which is why keeping an eye on it matters.",
      "noticed": [
        "A hard lump or ridge under the skin of the palm, below the ring or little finger; a new lump can be a little tender for a few weeks",
        "A tight cord you can feel when you try to straighten the finger, and a finger that stops short of straight",
        "Usually painless; sometimes itching or a pulling feeling",
        "Trouble laying the hand flat, putting it in a pocket or glove, washing your face, shaking hands, or catching the bent finger on things",
        "It changes slowly over months or years rather than flaring; sometimes both hands, and firm pads over the knuckles or lumps in the sole of the foot",
        "More common over 50, in men, with a family history or Northern European ancestry, and with diabetes, smoking or heavy alcohol use"
      ],
      "homeCare": [
        "Check your hand flat on a table every few months; if a finger starts to lift, book a review rather than waiting",
        "Keep using your hand normally: activity does not make the cord grow faster, and keeping the fingers moving keeps the rest of the hand healthy",
        "Stretching or forcing the finger straight does not stop the cord, so there is no need to push it",
        "After a procedure, do your exercises little and often, keep the hand raised when it swells, and keep the wound clean and dry until it has healed; cutting down smoking and good diabetes control help healing"
      ],
      "seePhysioIf": [
        "You have found a lump or cord in your palm and want to understand it, and what to watch for",
        "A finger has started to bend and you would like help deciding on the next step: if the hand no longer lies flat, we will also suggest you see your family doctor about a hand-surgeon referral",
        "You are having, or have had, a release procedure and want a hand-therapy plan: swelling control, early movement, scar care, and a graded return to strength, work and hobbies"
      ],
      "clinicNotes": [
        "Physio role: education and monitoring; timely referral (GP to hand surgeon) once the hand no longer lies flat; hand therapy before and after fasciectomy, needle fasciotomy or injection. Stretching, splinting and manual therapy do not prevent progression (NICE IPG43 / BSSH); do not sell a course of treatment to \"prevent\" it.",
        "Assess: Hueston table-top test; passive extension deficit at MCP and PIP by goniometry, each joint; Tubiana stage; nodule vs cord, skin pitting; diathesis (bilateral, early onset, family history, Garrod's pads, plantar nodules) means higher recurrence.",
        "Referral threshold (Dutch guideline 2023; HAND-2): MCP 30 degrees or more, or any PIP contracture, with functional loss. PIP contractures respond less well, so refer them earlier.",
        "Differentiate: trigger finger (A1 nodule, locking), ulnar neuropathy (Froment, intrinsic wasting, sensation), cervical screen, camptodactyly (since childhood, both hands, no cord), post-traumatic or arthritic joint stiffness, ganglion or callus; a rapidly growing, painful or deep lump needs a doctor.",
        "Procedures: needle fasciotomy (best for MCP; recurrence about 60% at 3 years), limited fasciectomy (standard; about 1 in 5 recur within 5 years), dermofasciectomy; collagenase injection availability varies in Canada.",
        "After surgery: wound and dehiscence, oedema (circumferential), active and passive range, total active motion, tendon glides, scar pliability, digital nerve sensation, grip and pinch from 6 weeks, QuickDASH or MHQ, early CRPS screen (Budapest). Night extension splint only if extension is being lost: routine splinting adds nothing to hand therapy (Jerosch-Herold 2011 SCoRD; Collis 2013; Kemler 2012). Graded strengthening from about 4 to 6 weeks.",
        "Sources: Dutch multidisciplinary guideline on Dupuytren disease (J Hand Surg Am 2023); NICE IPG43 / BSSH; Dias 2024 (HAND-2 protocol, Trials); Jerosch-Herold 2011; Collis 2013; Kemler 2012; Aggarwal and Blazar 2025 (UpToDate); AIM Theory Manual 2023 Ch. 2.9."
      ]
    },
